%%(activator),
%%constituting a self-regulating process that may
%yielding a co-continuous mixture of activate and inhibited response.
However, although the mechanisms of pattern formation are broadly understood, the complex dynamics occurring across disparate spatio-temporal scales within an arbitrary system
%-- originating at the component (e.g., molecular) level --
often pose limitations to theoretical understanding and, thus, to practical development.
Typically, the theoretical analysis proceeds from analytical models derived phenomenologically rather than from physical laws, which obscures the link between small-scale interactions/processes and the observed macroscopic performance.
Moreover, the conditions for pattern formation are often difficult to satisfy and custom patterning-capable systems difficult to engineer.
%, or to manipulate \red{toward a more favorable set}.
%and the system parameters difficult to manipulate toward a custom response.
