Figure~\ref{fig:da-scaling} reports the analysis of the DA simulations using the models described in Eq.~\ref{eq:DAmodel}. The tracking studies are performed using a model of the LHC that comprises sixty realisations of the non-linear magnetic field errors and computing the orbits up to \num{1e6} turns. For each realisation, the DA is computed using the information from the numerical data, and the DA model is fitted using the same numerical data. However, the fit is performed using a subset of the available data, namely, selecting only the information up to \num{1e4} or \num{1e5} turns (left and right columns, respectively). The DA models are then used to predict the DA for \num{1e6}, \num{1e7}, and \num{1e8} (the last two values are beyond computing capabilities). The predictive power of the models is compared with the DA of numerical simulations up to \num{1e6} turns, and the agreement is excellent, of the order of 2\%-4\%. The data presented in the first row are based on the simplified DA model, whereas those presented in the second row are based on the Lambert-function model. These plots serve as outstanding illustrations of the effectiveness of the method based on DA scaling laws.
