\caption{The period tripling method and the origin of the second transition. (a) An illustration of how the disorder is individually added to one site in a $3\times 3$ supercell. The red dashed lines show the $3\times 3$ supercell. The color and size of the holes represent the local potential change. Only $4\times4$ period-tripled supercells are shown. (b) The Fourier component $\left|\tilde{V}(\mathbf{k})\right|^2$ of the structure in (a). (c) The relation between the iso-frequency contour (green circle) and the scattering-free region (red dashed circles) when $k<\frac{\pi}{3a}-\frac{K}{2}$. The iso-frequency contour doesn't contact a second scattering-free region. (d) The relation between the iso-frequency contour (blue circle) and the scatter-free region (red dashed circles) when $k>\frac{\pi}{3a}-\frac{K}{2}$. The iso-frequency contour is partially blocked by a second scattering-free region.}
\label{figs4}
