\subsection{Related Work}

%We give a brief overview of the most important techniques available for  minor-free graph metrics.
Apart from the ones used in this work, there are more VC set systems considered in the context of minor-free graph metrics
%Apart from the VC set systems considered by Li and Parter~\cite{li2019planar}, there are other constructions relying on  VC set systems
~\cite{bousquet2015vc, braverman2021coresets, ChepoiEV07, Cohen-AddadD0SS25, ducoffe2022diameter, KarczmarzZ25}.
%Another metric parameter bounded in minor-free metrics is the {\em scatter dimension} \cite{BourneufP25}.
Some other approaches to such metrics
are based on a decomposition into low-diameter subgraphs, including the classic KPR theorem~\cite{KPR93}.
Our low-diameter clustering scheme %resembles
bears resemblance to 
the {\em shortcut partitions}~\cite{chang2023covering, chang2023resolving, chang2024shortcut} which provide even stronger guarantees but it is not known how to construct them in almost-linear time.
The related {\em buffered cop decompositions}~\cite{chang2024shortcut} yield cluster graphs of bounded weak coloring numbers~\cite{BourneufP25}.
These in turn are connected to {\em sparse covers}~\cite{abraham2007strong, busch2007improved, Filtser2004sparse}, however there the low-diameter subgraphs need not to be disjoint.

An alternative way to represent a metric by a bounded treewidth graph is a~{\em metric embedding}.
For example, an edge-weighted planar graph $G$ can be embedded into a metric given by a graph of treewidth $f(\eps)$ so that the additive distortion is $\eps \cdot \diam(G)$~\cite{FoxEpsteinKS19}.
The function $f$ can be estimated as $f(\eps) = \Oh(\eps^{-4})$~\cite{chang2023covering}.
For apex-minor-free graphs, the current best treewidth bound is $f(\eps) = 2^{\Oh(1/\eps)}$~\cite{chang2024shortcut}.
In the latter case, we again do not know if such an embedding can be computed in almost-linear time.
See also \cite{CohenAddadLPP23, Arnold22} for metric embeddings of minor-free graphs and a variant with multiplicative distortion. 

For more  advances in diameter computation, see \cite{ChanSkrepetos2019, chang24, duraj2023better}, and for recent developments in planar distance oracles, see \cite{ChangKT22, charalampopoulos2023almost, fredslundhansen, gawrychowski2018better, Le23}.
