\centering\includegraphics[width=12cm]{oracle.pdf}
\caption{Illustration to \Cref{claim:inner-path}.
By inspecting the nodes $t \in V(T)$ that are ancestral to both $\loc(u),\loc(v)$, we obtain a family of subgraphs $H_1 \subset H_2 \subset H_3 \subset H_4 \subset H_5 = G$, induced by the sets $V_{t}$.
Here, $H_4$ is the smallest among them that accommodates the shortest $(u,v)$-path $Q$. This path must intersect some cluster $C_i$ for which $\loc(i) = t$, where $G[V_t] = H_4$, as otherwise it would be contained in $H_3$.
Hence the $(u,v)$-distance in $H_4$ is the same as in $G$ and we can stretch $Q$ within $H_4$ to go through $c_i$ using \Cref{lem:path-touch}.
