\section{Technical Overview}

We first discuss the details of Theorems \ref{thm:first} and \ref{thm:oracle}.
We give a unified description since their proofs share several common steps.

\subparagraph{Cluster graphs of low treewidth.}
Our main tool will be a clustering $\ccal$ of a graph $G$ into induced subgraphs of strong diameter $\delta$, such that the cluster graph $G_\ccal$, obtained by contracting each cluster into a single vertex, maps vertices $u,v$ 
into clusters of (unweighted) distance proportional to $d_G(u,v) / \delta$.
This bears resemblance to {\em shortcut partitions} from X, however we need a weaker condition and so we are able to construct such a clustering in almost-linear time.
To this end, we fix any root vertex $r$ and compute the shortest-paths tree $T$ rooted at $r$.
Then we group the vertices into layers so that $\layer(v)$ is the largest integer $j$ for which $j\cdot \delta \le d_G(r,v)$.
Next, we contract all the edges in $T$ connecting vertices of the same layer.

For the sake of estimating diameter we are interested in the case $\delta = \eps\cdot\diam(G)$.
Then each root-to-leaf path in $T$ goes through at most $1/\eps$ layers and so the (unweighted) diameter of $G_\ccal$ is at most  $2/\eps$.
When $G$ excludes an apex graph $H$ as a minor,
then the same holds for $G_\ccal$ because it is a contraction of $G$.
Now we can take advantage of the local treewidth property to say that $\tw(G_\ccal) = \Oh_H(1/\eps)$.

This is insufficient though for the design of a $(1+\eps)$-approximate distance oracle because we need to estimate $d_G(u,v)$ also when it is way smaller than $\diam(G)$.
To address this, we will build a separate oracle for each scale and employ the technique of {\em low treewidth coloring} (X).
Suppose that $d_G(u,v) \in [\Delta / 2, \Delta]$ and consider a clustering $\ccal$ for $\delta = \eps\Delta$.
By the triangle inequality, the distances from $r$ to $u$ and $v$ cannot differ by more than $\Delta$ so $|\layer(u) - \layer(v)| \le 1/\eps.$
Furthermore, every vertex lying on a shortest $(u,v)$-path must have received a nearby layer.
Consequently, the $(u,v)$-distance is preserved in the  subgraph induced by $\Oh(1/\eps)$ consecutive layers and the corresponding cluster graph can be again shown to have bounded treewidth.
It is then easy to argue that one can preserve distances at scale $\Delta$ by replacing $G$ with a family a subgraphs of this form, so that each vertex appears in only $\Oh(1/\eps)$ subgraphs, which leads to a bound on the total preprocessing time.

We have so far reduced both problems to the case where the cluster graph $G_\ccal$ has treewidth $k = \Oh_H(1/\eps)$.
For the next step, we need to construct its tree decomposition of moderate width.
Whereas most almost-linear algorithms for this task require time exponential in $k$, 
we can use the algorithm from~\cite{FominLSPW18} that produces a tree decomposition of width $\Oh(k^2)$ in time $\Oh(k^7\cdot n\log n)$.
