\subparagraph{Processing a tree decomposition.}

While several algorithms rely on dynamic programming over a tree decomposition to compute the diameter exactly~\cite{AbboudWW16, Eppstein00, Husfeldt16}, we cannot afford such an approach due to prohibitive error accumulation.
Instead, we turn the decomposition into a balanced one (so that its depth is $\Oh(\log n)$) by increasing its width slightly~\cite{chatterjee2014optimal}.

We focus first on constructing the oracle as it will later help in estimating the eccentricities.
Let $C_1,\dots,C_k$ be the set of clusters appearing in the root bag of the decomposition.
In each $C_i$ we fix some {\em center} $c_i \in C_i$ and for each $i \in [k]$ we compute all the $(c_i,v)$-distance using the linear single-source shortest paths (SSSP) algorithm for minor-free graphs~\cite{TazariM09}.
Next, we recurse into the connected components of $G - \bigcup C_i$.
By adapting the argument from~\cite{Thorup04}\micr{check. this is what is said in \cite{abraham2006object}} we will argue that the distances computed this way suffice to quickly recover every $(u,v)$-distance approximately.

Consider vertices $u,v$ and some shortest $(u,v)$-path $P$.
Let $G_1 \subset G_2 \subset \dots \subset G_\ell$ be the sequence of induced subgraphs containing both $u,v$, given by backtracking the recursion; note that $\ell = \Oh(\log n)$.
Let $i$ be the smallest index for which $P$ is contained in $G_i$.
Then $P$ must intersect some cluster $C_j$ in the corresponding bag and it can be stretched slightly to pass through $c_j$.
It is crucial that the clusters have bounded {\em strong} diameter so this operation can be performed within the graph $G_i$.
