A similar issue is handled in \cite{li2019planar} via randomization\micr{check} but we can afford a simpler argument because we do not work in a distributed regime.



\subparagraph{Beyond Apex-Minor-Free Graphs.}
Consider an apex graph with apex $v$.
No longer local treewidth.
But compute all the distances from $v$ and assign $1/ \eps$ labels.
We have relevant pairs and relevant labels.
Each relevant label must be contained in a small ball in $G-v$.
Now consider irrelevant $u$ far from the pegs.
Any shortest path that goes through $u$ either goes through $v$ or connect two irrelevant guys, so we can remove $u$.
Then $G-v$ can be covered by $1/ \eps$ balls and so it is a union of low diameter graphs, at most $\diam(G) / \eps$.
Now we can use local treewidth, after contraction tw $\le 1/ \eps^2$, compute the tree decomposition and connect them by $v$.
Note that this no longer works for eccentricities.
