\begin{abstract}
  We consider a Bayesian diffusion control problem of expected terminal utility maximization. The controller imposes a prior distribution on the unknown drift of an underlying diffusion. The Bayesian optimal control, tracking the posterior distribution of the unknown drift, can be characterized explicitly. However, in practice, the prior will generally be incorrectly specified, and the degree of model misspecification can have a significant impact on policy performance. To mitigate this and reduce overpessimism, we introduce a distributionally robust Bayesian control (DRBC) formulation in which the controller plays a game against an adversary who selects a prior in divergence neighborhood of a baseline prior. The adversarial approach has been studied in economics \citep{hansen2008robustness} and efficient algorithms have been proposed in static optimization settings \citep{rahimian2019distributionally}. We develop a strong duality result for our DRBC formulation. Combining these results together with tools from stochastic analysis, we are able to derive a loss that can be efficiently trained (as we demonstrate in our numerical experiments) using a suitable neural network architecture. As a result, we obtain an effective algorithm for computing the DRBC optimal strategy. The methodology for computing the DRBC optimal strategy is greatly simplified, as we show, in the important case in which the adversary chooses a prior from a Kullback-Leibler distributional uncertainty set. 
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
We study a diffusion control problem for which a controller aims at maximizing expected terminal utility by making decisions informed by observations. Since the controller cannot directly observe model parameters or the driving factors, it is natural to consider a Bayesian approach to learn while optimizing, simultaneously. Thus, the unknown parameter or factor is modeled as an unobservable random element with a prior distribution. This approach, known as Bayesian control, is well studied in the control literature (see \cite{KZ98} for an example) and gives rise to sophisticated policies that naturally work well if the full Bayesian model is well specified (i.e. it matches reality).

However, if the model is not well specified, the Bayesian policy will often deliver suboptimal results. Adversarial approaches have been used to mitigate the impact of model misspecification. The controller interacts with a fictitious adversary to maximize value function, while the adversary selects a worst-case probability to ensure policy robustness. Distributionally robust control (DRC) \citep{HansenSargent2001, hansen2008robustness} is one of such method, and it is built on finding robust formulations that are tractable in the sense of leading to a dynamic programming principle. In exchange of this type of tractability, the approach leads to very pessimistic policies, because the adversary's power is replenished at every point in time. This also makes calibrating the size of the distributional uncertainty difficult, because small variations on this parameter have a significant impact on performance.

To address over-conservatism, we consider a distributionally robust Bayesian
control (DRBC) formulation in which we only build distributional robustness around the prior distribution in the Bayesian control formulation. This allows us to combat pessimistic policies at the expense of loosing the dynamic programming principle, yet we need to develop alternative methods for computing the DRBC optimal policy. 
In this paper, we illustrate how to overcome the challenge of computing an optimal DRBC policy in the context of a Bayesian diffusion control problem in an important example in dynamic portfolio optimization \citep{Merton1971, KZ98} where drift is unknown. 


\subsection{Our Contributions}


Our contributions are summarized as follows. 
\begin{itemize}
    \item We formulate a DRBC problem for misspecified priors in the context of diffusion control using a $\phi$-divergence uncertainty set around the prior (which is imposed on the drift of the diffusion) (Section \ref{amset}).
    \item We develop a strong duality result which shows that the problem is related to smooth ambiguity in financial mathematics, thus connecting with that literature (Theorem \ref{duality}).
    \item We provide sample complexity results showing that policy evaluation is possible with the canonical rate $\mathcal{O}_p\left(n^{-1/2}\right)$ via a novel randomized multi-level Monte Carlo (rMLMC) unbiased estimator (Algorithm \ref{DROPE} and Theorem \ref{CLT}). 
    \item We introduce a class of policies that contains the optimal policy for a large class of utility maximization problems of interest in the Bayesian control case and that are learnable efficiently via deep learning methods (Theorem \ref{system}). In the important special case of KL divergence, we find semi-closed form expressions to simplify the learning (Section \ref{toy}).
    \item We present simulation results to demonstrate the accuracy of our deep learning method (Section \ref{smps}) and confirm the $\mathcal{O}_p\left(n^{-1/2}\right)$ convergence rate (Section \ref{CLTnum}). Moreover, numerical results show the robustness of DRBC and it overcomes overpessimism (Section \ref{fulldroexp}).
\end{itemize}


\subsection{Related Work}
\label{relatedwork}

\textbf{Distributionally Robust Methods} have been well-studied in a wide range of areas. For example, Distributionally Robust Optimization (DRO) (i.e. the supervised learning case) can be shown to recover a wide range of successful statistical estimators (including sqrt-Lasso, AdaBoost, group Lasso etc.), by carefully choosing the uncertainty set, often in terms of $\phi$-divergence or Wasserstein sets (see \cite{Blanchet2021WassersteinDRO, Blanchet2024DRO}). Refer also to \cite{rahimian2019distributionally, Bayraksan2015} for comprehensive reviews on DRO. 

%\citep{JohnDuchi} and Wasserstein balls \citep{Abadeh2018, Blanchet2019, Gao2016}.  

Motivated by problems in areas such as economics and finance, among others, \citep{HansenSargent2001, hansen2008robustness, RB1, RB2} , DRO has been generalized to the setting of dynamic decision-making with model uncertainty. This situation is significantly more complicated and the literature has focused mostly on developing formulations that are amenable to dynamic programming (DP), giving rise to Distributionally Robust Control (DRC) and Distributionally Robust Markov Decision Processes (DRMDP). The availability of a dynamic programming principle facilitates the development of Distributionally Robust Reinforcement Learning (DRRL) and related settings \citep{NianSi, wang2022policy,Wang2023, Liu2022,Zhou2021, lu2024drrl}. 

Unfortunately, to develop a DP in the DRC, DRMDP, and DRRL settings, the adversary gets its power replenished at every point in time, making the formulations pessimistic. That occurs at every point in time, thus making these formulations overconservative. 

\textit{In contrast, our formulation combines Bayesian stochastic control with DRO by introducing a single distributional uncertainty set in the prior distribution. This combats overconservative solutions, but at the expense of the DP. However, we develop a formulation and techniques that make the optimal solution learnable by exploiting continuous time stochastic analysis. }

Computations of static DRO problems has been studied by \cite{levy2020large, blanchet2020semi, wang2021sinkhorn}. Compared with us, they consider different uncertainty sets and the estimators are biased.
 We also mention the literature on RL in finance \citep{Hambly2023}, Bayesian Optimization \citep{Daulton22} and Distributionally Robust Bayesian Optimization (DRBO, \citep{pmlr-v108-kirschner20a}). Our setting is different from DRBO. We work in continuous time, which allows us to use stochastic analysis (via the martingale method and other techniques) to obtain convenient expressions to define a suitable loss for the optimal strategy. Our formulation is also offline and not online as in DRBO. We wish to efficiently evaluate policies for the game corresponding to our formulation. In addition, the work on Bayesian Distributionally Robust Optimization (BDRO, \cite{doi:10.1137/21M1465548}) is more related to our setting here, but it focuses on a static setting. 

\section{Settings and Formulations}\label{set}
% \subsection{Notations}
\subsection{Classical Bayesian Case}
Let $(\Omega, \mathcal{F}, P)$ be a complete filtered probability space, and $W$ is a standard Brownian motion under $P$. $B: \Omega \to \mathbb{R}$ is a real-valued random variable such that $B$ and $W$ are independent. We denote $\sigma(B)$ as the $\sigma$-algebra generated by $B$ and assume that $B$ has a distribution $\mu \in \mathcal{P}(\mathbb{R})$ (the space of all Borel probability measures on $\mathbb{R}$). 
We have the risk-free asset $S_{0,0} = s_0 > 0$ and $
d S_{0, t} = r S_{0, t} d t, \;\; 0 \leq t \leq T,
$with interest-free rate $r> 0$ and a risky asset $S$ with drift $B$
\begin{equation}\label{stock}
 d S_t = S_t (B d t + \sigma d W_t),\;\; 0 \leq t \leq T.   
\end{equation}
% Unlike the classical Merton's model \citep{Merton1971} in Black-Scholes market which assumes the drift of the stock price process is a known constant, we model it as a random variable since in reality it is hard to be estimated from real financial data and we want to pose prior information on it and utilize Bayesian analysis and filtering techniques.
We next define filtrations: $\mathcal{F}^S$ is the natural filtration of $S$, and $\mathcal{G}$ is the union of $\sigma(B)$ and $\mathcal{F}^W$ (the natural filtration of $W$). 
% Hence, for any $t \in [0,T]$, $\mathcal{F}^S_t \subset \mathcal{G}_t \subset \mathcal{F}$.
We denote the $\mathcal{F}^S$-adapted (the decisions are made based on only the observations of the stock prices) stochastic process $\pi = \{\pi_t\}_{t \in [0,T]}$ as the amount of money invested in the risky asset. This induces the dynamics of a controlled wealth process with $X_0 = x_0$ (we simplify the notation so that $X^{\pi}$ is written as $X$)
\begin{equation}\label{wealthp}
		d X_t = (X_t - \pi_t) r \, d t + \pi_t \left(B d t + \sigma d W_t\right).
\end{equation}
In this paper, control, policy, and strategy are used interchangeably. We call an $\mathcal{F}^S$-progressively measurable stochastic processes (control) $\pi= \{\pi_t\}_{t \in [0,T]}$ admissible if $X_0 = x_0$, $\int_0^T \left \Vert \pi_t \right\Vert_2^2dt < \infty$, and 
     Equation (\ref{wealthp}) admits a unique strong solution. The collection of all admissible controls is denoted as  $\mathcal{A}(x_0)$.
The Bayesian diffusion control problem is defined as
\begin{equation}\label{OriginalKaratzas}
  V(x_0) = \sup_{\pi \in \mathcal{A}(x_0)}E^P\left[u(X_T)\right],  
\end{equation}
where the utility function $u: (0, \infty) \to \mathbb{R}$ is strictly concave and strictly increasing. Without loss of generality, in this paper, we consider the utility function $u(x) = \frac{1}{\alpha}x^{\alpha}$ with $\alpha \in (0,1)$ and $X_t >0$ for any $t \in [0,T]$. 
The optimal solution of Problem (\ref{OriginalKaratzas}) is given by Theorem \ref{Karatzassolution} in Appendix \ref{mgmethod} \citep{KZ98}. The takeaway is that the optimal solution $V(x_0)$ is a functional of the prior distribution $\mu$, and the optimal policy is a random variable depending on $\mu$ and the observations. In practice, the prior distribution is chosen by experts and other available information, and in reality the fraction of investment into risky asset is computed via the formula provided by Theorem \ref{Karatzassolution} with real observations.

\subsection{Ambiguity Set for the Distributionally Robustness}\label{amset}
Given a convex function $ \phi: [0, \infty) \rightarrow \mathbb{R} $ with $\phi(1)= 0 $, $\phi$-divergence of $ Q$ from $ P $ is $D_\phi(P \| Q) = \int_\Omega \phi \left( \frac{dP}{dQ} \right) dQ,$
where $\frac{dP}{dQ}$ is the Radon-Nikodym derivative of $P$ with respect to $Q$. If $\phi(x) = x\log(x) -x + 1$, then $D_{\phi}$ is the Kullback–Leibler (KL) divergence, denoted as $D_{\text{KL}}$.

In the Bayesian problem (\ref{OriginalKaratzas}), the imposed prior may not be exactly the same as the underlying drift. Distributionally Robust Control (DRC) methods relying on dynamic programming principles to mitigate this model misspecification \citep{HansenSargent2001} are often too pessimistic since in every step the worst case is chosen. Thus we consider a distributionally robust Bayesian control (DRBC) formulation where the controller engages in a game against an adversary who chooses a prior for the drift at the beginning from a $\phi$-divergence neighborhood (we call this ambiguity set or uncertainty set) around the baseline prior to overcome this overpessimism.
To rigorously define the uncertainty set, we need to make sure only distribution of $B$ is changed and all other conditions (e.g. measurability) are kept the same. We denote $\mathcal{P}(\Omega, \mathcal{F})$ as the collection of all Borel probability measures on the measurable space $(\Omega, \mathcal{F})$. Then we define the following set $(B \perp W$ means that the two random variables $B$ and $W$ are independent)
\begin{equation*}
\mathcal{Q}_\delta 
= \left\{\begin{aligned}
  &Q \in \mathcal{P}(\Omega, \mathcal{F}), \\ &Q \ll P
\end{aligned}
\;\middle|\;
\begin{aligned}
    & Q(B \in A) = \nu(A) \text{ for some }  \nu \in \mathcal{P}(\mathbb{R}), \forall A \in \mathcal{B}(\mathbb{R}),  D_{\phi}(\nu \| \mu) \leq \delta, \\ 
    & B \perp W,  W \text{ is a standard Brownian} \, \text{motion under } Q.
\end{aligned}
\right\}.
\end{equation*}

The set $\mathcal{Q}_\delta$ is endowed with the topology of weak convergence \citep{billingsley1986probability}. We define a relation on $\mathcal{Q}_\delta$: 
% \begin{equation}\label{relation}
%     Q_1 \sim Q_2 \text{ if and only if for any }A \in \mathcal{B}(\mathbb{R}), Q_1(B \in A) = Q_2(B \in A). 
% \end{equation}
\begin{equation}\label{relation}
Q_1 \sim Q_2 \iff 
\begin{aligned}
    & \text{for any } A \in \mathcal{B}(\mathbb{R}),  Q_1(B \in A) = Q_2(B \in A).
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
We use the quotient space  $\mathcal{U}_\delta =  \mathcal{Q}_\delta / \sim$ as the uncertainty set for the DRBC problem. The following theorem gives an intuition of this.

\begin{theorem}\label{uncertaintyset}
     $\mathcal{U}_\delta$ is well-defined.
     For all $Q \in \mathcal{U}_\delta$, there exists $\nu \ll \mu$ such that $\frac{dQ}{dP} = \frac{d\nu}{d\mu}(B)$ $P$-almost surely and $D_{\phi}\left(Q \parallel P\right) = D_{\phi}\left(\nu \parallel \mu\right)$. 
       % \item (3) If, then for any $Q_{\delta} \in \mathcal{U}_{\delta}$, $Q_{\delta} \to P$ weakly as $\delta \to 0$. 

\end{theorem}

With Theorem \ref{uncertaintyset}, we define the goal of the DRBC problem 
\begin{equation}\label{DRO}
   \sup_{\pi \in \mathcal{A}(x_0)} 
\inf_{Q \in \mathcal{U}_{\delta}} E^Q\left[u(X_T)\right]. 
\end{equation}We denote the optimal solution of Problem (\ref{DRO}) as $\pi_{\text{DRBC}}$. Compared with the DRC approach, Problem (\ref{DRO}) looses the tractability in terms of dynamic programming principle (see Appendix \ref{Shengbo}), thus we need to develop another method to compute $\pi_{\text{DRBC}}$. The first step is to use duality to simplify the representation of the original problem.

\section{Strong Duality}\label{dual}
We begin with some notations. Suppose $f: \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ is a real-valued function, we denote $f^*$ as the convex conjugate of $f$, which is defined as $f^*(x^*) = \sup_{x \in X} \left( \langle x^*, x \rangle - f(x) \right) = -\inf_{x \in X} \left( f(x) - \langle x^*, x \rangle \right)$ and $\langle ., . \rangle$ is the standard inner product in $\mathbb{R}$. Moreover, there exists unique class of probability measures $\{P^b\}_{b \in \mathbb{R}} \subset \mathcal{P}(\Omega, \mathcal{F})$ such that for any $A \in \mathcal{F}$,
$P(A) = \int_{\mathbb{R}}P^b(A)d\mu(b).$ For a matrix $\boldsymbol{A}$ (could be reduced to a vector), $\boldsymbol{A}^T$ denotes its transpose and $\boldsymbol{A}^{-1}$ denotes its inverse if exists.
We then prove an extension of the strong duality result in \cite{Shapiro17}, which transforms the original infinite-dimensional problem to finite-dimensional.
% Theorem \ref{duality} can be viewed as a conditional version of the strong duality result obtained in \citet{Shapiro17}. Indeed, we develop the conditional version of the law invariance theory.

% \textcolor{red}{Be careful about the case when $\lambda = 0$. May require new definitions}
\begin{theorem}\label{duality}
    \begin{align*}
      &\inf_{Q \in \mathcal{U}_{\delta}} E^Q\left[u(X_T)\right] =\sup_{\lambda \geq 0, \beta \in \mathbb{R}}\left\{\beta - \lambda \delta +  \int_{\mathbb{R}}\Phi_{\lambda,\beta}\left(E^{P^b}\left[u(X_T)\right]\right)d\mu(b)\right\},
    \end{align*}
    where for fixed $\lambda \geq 0, \beta \in \mathbb{R}$, 
    $$\Phi_{\lambda,\beta}(x):= -(\lambda\phi)^*(\beta-x).$$
    % Moreover, if the infimum on the left hand side is finite, then there exist(s) (may not be unique) $\lambda(\alpha) \geq 0$ and $\beta(\alpha) \in \mathbb{R}$ attaining the supremum on the right hand side.
\end{theorem} In general, as a function of $x$, $\Phi_{\lambda, \beta}$ is always concave due to the convexity of $\phi$, but it is not always increasing. As a function of $(\lambda, \beta)^T$, $\Phi_{\lambda, \beta}$ is always concave. For the convenience of the notations, we denote $Z^b := E^{P^b}\left[u(X_T)\right]$.
If $\phi$ induces the KL divergence, then we denote the uncertainty set as $\mathcal{U}_{\text{KL},\delta}$ and have the following duality result.
\begin{theorem}\label{KLduality}
 \begin{align*}
     &\inf_{Q \in \mathcal{U}_{\text{KL},\delta}} E^Q\left[u(X_T)\right] =\sup_{\lambda \geq 0}\left\{-\lambda \delta -\lambda\log\left(\int_{\mathbb{R}}\exp\left(\frac{-Z^b }{\lambda}\right)d\mu(b)\right)\right\},
 \end{align*}where  $\lambda \mapsto -\lambda \delta -\lambda\log\left(\int_{\mathbb{R}}\exp\left(\frac{-Z^b }{\lambda}\right)d\mu(b)\right)$ takes the value $\text{ess inf }Z^B$ when $\lambda = 0$ and ess inf denotes the essential infimum.
 \end{theorem}
The analysis of an extension which is called Cressie-Read divergence is provided in Appendix \ref{C-R}.
We give assumptions on the $\phi$-divergence to ensure the attainability of an optimal pair $\left(\lambda^*, \beta^*\right)^T = (\lambda^*(\pi), \beta^*(\pi))^T$ with $\lambda^*(\pi) > 0$ for a fixed $\pi \in \mathcal{A}(x_0)$. The precise discussion can be found in Appendix \ref{upperboundsec}. We notice that the duality can be further written as a univariate function $\Phi_{\lambda}$ or $\Phi_{\beta}$ in terms of the dual variable (as in Theorem \ref{KLduality}). For the notational convenience, if $\Phi_{\lambda}$ or $\Phi_{\beta}$ is strictly concave in $\lambda$ or $\beta$, we still say $\Phi_{\lambda, \beta}$ is strictly concave. This will not affect the rate of the asymptotic analysis, and the impacts on explicit computation are discussed in Appendix \ref{upperboundsec} and \ref{derivatives}.

\begin{assumption}\label{ass0}
For a fixed control $\pi \in \mathcal{A}(x_0)$, the norm of the pair of the optimal multipliers $\left(\lambda^*, \beta^*\right)^T$ has finite upper and lower bounds. In particular, $\lambda^* \neq 0$.  
\end{assumption}

\begin{assumption}\label{ass0.5}
$\Phi_{\lambda, \beta}$ is strictly increasing and strictly concave in $x$, and it is strictly concave in $(\lambda, \beta)^T$. For any fixed $\lambda \geq 0$ and $\beta \in \mathbb{R}$, $\Phi_{\lambda, \beta}$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $Z^b$ $\mu$-almost surely, satisfies the linear growth condition, and $D\Phi_{\lambda, \beta}$ is locally H\"{o}lder continuous with parameter $w \geq 1$ and bounding constant $\mathcal{K}$. 
\end{assumption}

\begin{assumption}\label{ass2}
 For any fixed $b \in \mathbb{R}$, the value $V_b(x_0) = \sup_{\pi \in \mathcal{A}(x_0)}E^{P^b}\left[u(X_T)\right] < \infty$. This assumption is standard in portfolio optimizations problem \citep{Karatzas1991}. Moreover, we denote for a fixed control $\pi \in \mathcal{A}(x_0)$, $\text{ ess inf }X_T = m \geq 0$. We assume that $\Phi_{\lambda^*, \beta^*}(0) > 0$.
\end{assumption}

Both of the following assumptions will imply Assumption \ref{ass0} in the KL divergence case.

\begin{assumption}\label{ass3}
The prior $\mu$ is a light-tailed distribution such that $0 < M:=\int_{\mathbb{R}}V_b(x_0)^{3(1+w)}d\mu(b) < \infty$.    
\end{assumption}

% In particular, the commonly used compactly supported priors \citep{BGP19} will satisfy Assumption \ref{ass2} and \ref{ass3}. However, only compactly supportedness of the prior does not guarantee Assumption \ref{ass1}, which is a stronger assumption to ensure a lower bound of the optimal $\lambda^*(\pi)$. Assumption \ref{ass1} can be achieved for example when $\mu$ is finitely supported with positive probability for each point.

\begin{assumption}\label{ass1}
The density (or probability mass function) of $E^{P^B}[u(X_T)]$ has a uniform lower bound $b >0$ and the domain of density (or probability mass function) is compact.   
\end{assumption}
As mentioned in \cite{NianSi} and \cite{JohnDuchi}, Assumption \ref{ass0}, \ref{ass0.5}, and \ref{ass2} are satisfied with mild conditions for KL and Cressie-Read divergences. From strong duality, the general form of Problem (\ref{DRO}) becomes 
\begin{align*}
   & \sup_{\pi \in \mathcal{A}(x_0)} 
\inf_{Q \in \mathcal{U}_{\delta}} E^Q\left[u(X_T)\right] = \sup_{\pi \in \mathcal{A}(x_0)}\sup_{\substack{\lambda > 0 \\ \beta \in \mathbb{R}}}\left\{\beta - \lambda \delta +  \int_{\mathbb{R}}\Phi_{\lambda,\beta}\left(Z^b\right)d\mu(b)\right\}. 
% \\&=\sup_{\substack{\lambda > 0 \\ \beta \in \mathbb{R}}}\left\{\beta - \lambda \delta + \lambda \sup_{\pi \in \mathcal{A}(x_0)} \int_{\mathbb{R}}\Phi_{\lambda,\beta}\left(Z^b\right)d\mu(b)\right\}.
\end{align*}
Solving Problem (\ref{DRO}) is hard in general. We provide a heuristic example of closed-form computation in Appendix \ref{closed} to illustrate this difficulty. 
In particular, the difficulties are two-fold: the double supremum is highly nonconvex and even doing policy learning with fixed $\lambda$ and $\beta$ is hard. 
Inspired by the typical choices in distributionally robust contextual bandit \citep{NianSi},
 we use an alternative optimization algorithm to get the well-approximated optimal solution $\hat{\pi}_{\text{DRBC}} \approx \pi_{\text{DRBC}}$ (See Algorithm \ref{DROKLA} for the KL case). We learn $\hat{\pi}_{\text{DRBC}}$ by first fixing $\pi$ and optimizing $(\lambda, \beta)^T$ (we call this step policy evaluation) and then by fixing $(\lambda, \beta)^T$ and optimizing $\pi$ (we call this step policy learning). We continue this alternative updating until the values of $(\lambda, \beta)^T$ converges. Simulation results show that this alternative iterative algorithm works well in practice (see Section \ref{Sec6}).
% \section{Iterative Algorithm for Solving the Distributionally Robust Problem}\label{solver}


\section{Policy Evaluation Step}\label{outeroptimization}
We first fix a control $\pi \in \mathcal{A}(x_0)$ and aim to compute 
\begin{equation}\label{outer}
    Q_{\text{DRBC}}(\pi) = \sup_{\substack{\lambda > 0 \\ \beta \in \mathbb{R}}}\left\{\beta - \lambda \delta + \int_{\mathbb{R}}\Phi_{\lambda,\beta}\left(Z^b\right)d\mu(b)\right\}
\end{equation} for the general case, while for the KL divergence case, we have the policy evaluation
% \begin{align}\label{outerKL}
%    &Q_{\text{DRBCKL}}(\pi)\\ &=\sup_{\substack{\lambda > 0}}\left\{- \lambda \delta -\lambda\log\left(\int_{\mathbb{R}}\exp\left(\frac{-Z^b}{\lambda}\right)d\mu(b)\right)\right\}\notag 
% \end{align} 
$Q_{\text{DRBCKL}}(\pi)=\sup_{\substack{\lambda > 0}}\left\{- \lambda \delta -\lambda\log\left(\int_{\mathbb{R}}\exp\left(\frac{-Z^b}{\lambda}\right)d\mu(b)\right)\right\}$. The basic idea is to first derive an unbiased estimator for the nested expectations (the inner samples depend on the outer samples)$\int_{\mathbb{R}}\Phi_{\lambda,\beta}\left(Z^b\right)d\mu(b)$ and $\log\left(\int_{\mathbb{R}}\exp\left(\frac{-Z^b}{\lambda}\right)d\mu(b)\right)$ and then use the standard Newton-Raphson method or (stochastic) gradient descent methods to find the optimal $\lambda^*$ and $\beta^*$ (Theorem \ref{Hessian}, \ref{KLderivatives}).

We focus on the general case. Assume that for a fixed $\pi \in \mathcal{A}(x_0)$, we have the access to the simulator $\mathcal{S}$ which is able to generate samples from the distribution of $\mu$ and take one sample $b$ from $\mu$ as an input to generate unbiased (conditional) samples from $Z^b$. This is a standard assumption \citep{YG23}. For the KL divergence case, see the discussion and Algorithm \ref{KLevaluate} in Appendix \ref{derivatives}.

We quickly review an important method that we will use in the approximation of the solution.
Multi-level Monte Carlo (MLMC) methods are designed to reduce the total computational complexity in the Monte Carlo estimations \citep{Giles2008, acta15}. \cite{RheeGlynn2015} proposes a randomized MLMC (rMLMC), which in addition produces unbiased estimates. The rMLMC estimator is also used to produce unbiased estimates of single-layer nested expectations and solutions of stochastic optimization problems (among other problems) \citep{Y_b, Yanan}.

% \begin{remark}
%     For the KL divergence, we could make similar assumptions for nested expectation with more layers \citep{YG23}. However, we still use the general form and avoid the use of rMLMC with more layers. When the optimal multipliers converge, we can only use the optimal $\lambda^*$ for the computation in the next iteration (See Section \ref{toy}).
% \end{remark}

% \begin{remark}
%     We only assume that we have an access to the generation of nested samples without considering how are they generated. Indeed, the type of this generation affects the rate of asymptotic analysis (e.g. Central Limit Theorem). For example, if $X_T$ can only be approximated by the classical Euler's scheme, then samples of $X_T$ are biased; on the other hand, if we use the rMLMC method \citep{RheeGlynn2015, ZhengBlanchetGlynn2018} to create unbiased estimations of $X_T$, then the rate of convergence is not optimal (canonical). This will be taken care by a more complicated analysis.
% \end{remark}

In our case, the randomized  (rMLMC) estimator first samples $b \sim \mu$, then independently samples a random $N^b = \tilde{N}^b + n_0$, where $n_0$ is a fixed non-negative integer and $\tilde{N}^b \sim \text{Geo}(R)$ with $R \in \left(\frac{1}{2},\frac{3}{4}\right)$, finally generates $2^{N^b+1}$ unbiased estimates $\left\{\hat{Z}^b_i\right\}_{1 \leq i \leq 2^{N^b+1}}$ of $Z^b = E^{P^b}[u(X_T)]$, and the random estimator of $\int_{\mathbb{R}}\Phi_{\lambda,\beta}\left(Z^b\right)d\mu(b)$ is given by 
$\mathcal{E}^b = \mathcal{E}^b_{\lambda, \beta} = \frac{\Delta_{N^b}^b}{p(N^b)} + \Phi_{\lambda, \beta}\left(\frac{S^b_{2^{n_0}}}{2^{n_0}}\right)$, where $p(.)$ is the probability mass function of $N^b$ and $S^b_{l} = \sum_{i=1}^l \hat{Z}^b_i$. 
For $N^b \geq n_0$, we define 
$\Delta_{N^b}^b = \Phi_{\lambda,\beta}\left(\frac{S_{2^{N^b+1}}^b}{2^{N^b+1}}\right) - \frac{1}{2}\left(\Phi_{\lambda,\beta}\left(\frac{S^{O,b}_{2^{N^b}}}{2^{N^b}}\right) + \Phi_{\lambda,\beta}\left(\frac{S^{E,b}_{2^{N^b}}}{2^{N^b}}\right)\right),$
% \begin{align*}
%     \Delta_{N^b}^b &= \Phi_{\lambda,\beta}\left(\frac{S_{2^{N^b+1}}^b}{2^{N^b+1}}\right) \\
%     &- \frac{1}{2}\left(\Phi_{\lambda,\beta}\left(\frac{S^{O,b}_{2^{N^b}}}{2^{N^b}}\right) + \Phi_{\lambda,\beta}\left(\frac{S^{E,b}_{2^{N^b}}}{2^{N^b}}\right)\right),
% \end{align*}
where 
 $S^{O,b}_{l} = \sum_{i=1}^l \hat{Z}^{O,b}_i$ and $S^{E,b}_{l} = \sum_{i=1}^l \hat{Z}^{E,b}_{i}$ with $\left\{\hat{Z}^{E,b}_{i}\right\}_{1 \leq i \leq 2^{N^b+1}}$ and $\left\{\hat{Z}^{O,b}_i\right\}_{1 \leq i \leq 2^{N^b+1}}$ denotes the estimates indexed by even and odd values, respectively. Our overall estimator $\mathcal{E}$ is (for fixed $\pi$ and $\delta$)
\begin{align}
    \label{rMLMC}
    \mathcal{E} &= \mathcal{E}_{\pi, \delta, \lambda, \beta}= \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n \mathcal{E}^{b_i} =\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n \left(\frac{\Delta_{N^{b_i}}^{b_i}}{p(N^{b_i})} + \Phi_{\lambda, \beta}\left(\frac{S^{b_i}_{2^{n_0}}}{2^{n_0}}\right)\right)
    %,\notag
\end{align}given $n$ i.i.d. samples $\left\{b_i\right\}_{1 \leq i \leq n}$ generated from $\mu$. 
